  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at half height. It is steeper in x, so overshoot shows there first: with no noise, x swings grow once learning rate > 2/3. Each step moves by −learning rate × (slope + noise).",
    "Noise is Gaussian, centred on the true slope, with spread 1.5·√((64−b)/(63·b)) for batch size b, like sampling b of 64 points. It is exactly zero at 64. Fixed seed: Reset replays the same noise.",
    "Both arrows are drawn at 2× the length of the step they show (one scale for both). Orange is the mini-batch step the ball takes. Grey is −learning rate × true slope.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as soon as the loss exceeds 6 (diverged). The trail holds the start plus one point per step. Settings changed mid-run apply from the next step.",
    "New run, keep last path replays the bowl with later noise values and keeps the finished path as the faded ghost. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Lowest point: the ball sits on the drawn surface and is held at the top when it leaves the bowl. The Loss readout always shows the true value."
  ]
}